==========================================================================================================================
PART 0 -- the residual identity, derived and then verified numerically
==========================================================================================================================
    r = log g_obs - log[nu(g_bar/a_0) g_bar]   =>   dr/dlog a_0 = m,   dr/dlog M_bar = 1 + m
    so   d log a_0 / d log M_bar = (1+m)/m = LAMBDA  -- k02's amplification law, from the other side.
             y      m(y)       1+m    1/m (a_0 amplification of <r>)    LAMBDA
         0.020   -0.4655    0.5345                            -2.148    -1.148
         0.065   -0.4390    0.5610                            -2.278    -1.278
         0.200   -0.3965    0.6035                            -2.522    -1.522
         0.330   -0.3701    0.6299                            -2.702    -1.702
         1.000   -0.2910    0.7090                            -3.437    -2.437
         2.000   -0.2271    0.7729                            -4.403    -3.403
         5.000   -0.1338    0.8662                            -7.474    -6.474
  [PASS] k-hzs-0 the residual's two partial derivatives are verified against finite differences on the exact kernel: dr/dlog a_0 = m and dr/dlog M_bar = -(1+m) (the minus because raising M_bar raises the MODEL).  If either were mis-derived the whole script would mis-scale   (at y = 0.33: dr/dlog a_0 numeric -0.370061 vs m = -0.370061; dr/dlog M_bar numeric -0.629939 vs -(1+m) = -0.629939)

    AND THE POINT OF USING r AT ALL: it exists for every galaxy.  The a_0 inversion does not.
    Censoring fractions measured in k_high-z_floor_census: MUSE-DARK II 25%, RC100 22%, KROSS 31%,
    and the induced one-sided bias on the published levels is -0.14 to -0.43 dex.  r has none.

==========================================================================================================================
PART 1 -- the residual measured, and a_0 read from it WITHOUT censoring, survey by survey
==========================================================================================================================
    a_0 is the value at which the sample's residual is centred on zero -- solved EXACTLY by
    bisection, not linearised (the linearised form -<r>/<m> is wrong by a factor 3 for the
    gas-free samples, whose |<r>| reaches 0.5 dex).  Both the mean-zero and the robust
    median-zero solutions are given, because two samples carry heavy tails.
    sample                                      N   <z>   med y   mean r   sd r  MAD r   a0 mean-zero  a0 median-zero dex vs canon
    SPARC resolved g_bar (z=0)                139  0.00   0.207   +0.008  0.139  0.120      9.822e-11       1.061e-10       +0.021
    SPARC degraded to M_bar+R_e (z=0)         139  0.00   0.365   -0.126  0.189  0.149      3.674e-11       4.428e-11       -0.406
    MUSE-DARK II lensed (z 0.5-1.45)           95  0.98   0.330   -0.127  0.359  0.364      3.723e-11       5.088e-11       -0.400
    RC100 (z 0.6-2.5)                         100  1.59   2.030   +0.054  0.213  0.185      1.548e-10       1.517e-10       +0.218
    MSA-3D golden, stars only (z 0.6-1.7)      23  1.12   0.271   +0.547  0.190  0.082      1.835e-09       1.933e-09       +1.292
    KROSS stars only (z~0.9)                  381  0.88   1.178   -0.002  0.406  0.399      9.222e-11       1.507e-10       -0.006

    SAMPLES WITHOUT GAS IN THEIR TABLE (MSA-3D, KROSS) have g_bar as a LOWER bound, so their a_0 is
    an UPPER bound and is not comparable with the others -- flagged, not quietly stacked.

    (a_0 here is the CENSORING-FREE level: every galaxy contributes, including the ones that have
     no a_0 of their own.  Compare k03's inverting-subset median 1.10e-10 for MUSE-DARK II.)
  [PASS] k-hzs-1 THE CENSORING-FREE LEVEL, and it is a check that can fail: the uncensored z ~ 1 a_0 must be BELOW the inverting-subset median that k03 reported, because censoring removes the LOW-a_0 galaxies.  If it is not below, the censoring warning in the floor census was empty   (uncensored MUSE-DARK II a_0 = 3.723e-11 against k03's inverting-subset median 1.102e-10, i.e. -0.471 dex; canonical -0.400 dex, alt -0.482 dex; robust median-zero 5.088e-11 = -0.265 dex)
  [PASS] k-hzs-1b THE z = 0 CONTROL for the same estimator: run on SPARC's resolved rotation curves the residual estimator must return a_0 near the canonical footing, or the estimator itself is biased (this is the check that caught item 25's +0.0985 dex)   (SPARC resolved returns a_0 = 9.822e-11 = +0.021 dex from canonical, -0.061 from alt; degraded to high-z information the SAME galaxies give -0.406 dex -- the estimator's own floor)

==========================================================================================================================
PART 1b -- THE BIAS-MATCHED DIFFERENTIAL: a_0(z ~ 1) against a_0(z = 0) through the IDENTICAL
           estimator, with the identical information content.  This is the number that matters.
==========================================================================================================================
    The degraded z = 0 control returns a_0 far from canonical.  That is not a failure of the
    framework -- it is the ESTIMATOR's own bias, and it is measurable because SPARC can be read
    both ways.  Measured here, the bias is:
      log a_0(degraded) - log a_0(resolved) on the SAME 139 galaxies = -0.427 dex
      Its origin is one assumption: the degraded estimator puts ALL the baryons in one exponential
      disc of the stellar scale length, so the extended HI is placed too far inside, g_bar at 2 R_e
      is overstated, and LAMBDA ~ -1.5 turns that into a large negative a_0 offset.

    log a_0(z = 0.98, MUSE-DARK II) - log a_0(z = 0, SPARC degraded) = +0.0057 +- 0.1538 dex  (+0.04 sigma from zero)
      over a look-back of about 7.7 Gyr.  For scale, a_0 ~ c H(z) would give +0.241 dex.

    THE CONTROL THE MATCHING NEEDS: is that bias the same at MUSE-DARK II's gas fraction?
      SPARC median f_gas = 0.44;  MUSE-DARK II median f_gas = 0.70
           f_gas bin    N   a0 resolved   a0 degraded  bias (dex)
           0.00-0.35   57     1.085e-10     5.583e-11      -0.288
           0.35-0.55   26     8.818e-11     4.588e-11      -0.284
           0.55-0.75   31     1.046e-10     3.651e-11      -0.457
           0.75-1.01   25     8.641e-11     1.630e-11      -0.724
  [FAIL] k-hzs-1c THE MATCHING CONTROL, and it is what decides whether the differential means anything: the estimator's bias is measured in bins of gas fraction at z = 0.  The check is that it is STABLE -- if the bias runs with f_gas, then matching a low-f_gas local sample to a high-f_gas high-z one is invalid and the differential is a gas-fraction difference wearing redshift's clothes   (bias across f_gas bins = -0.288, -0.284, -0.457, -0.724; spread 0.441 dex (gate 0.15))

    ^ THE UNMATCHED DIFFERENTIAL IS THEREFORE INVALID and must not be quoted.  SPARC's median
      f_gas is 0.44 and MUSE-DARK II's is 0.70, and the bias at those two gas fractions differs
      by about 0.17 dex.  The fix is to MATCH the local sample's gas-fraction distribution to the
      high-z one and redo the differential -- done next, because the control demanded it.

    THE GAS-FRACTION-MATCHED DIFFERENTIAL (SPARC reweighted, bin by bin, to MUSE-DARK II's own
    f_gas histogram; this is the only version that may be quoted):
      matched local a_0 = 1.130e-11  (effective N = 46.6 of 139; 100% of the high-z f_gas distribution has local counterparts)
      log a_0(z = 0.98) - log a_0(z = 0, f_gas-matched) = +0.5178 +- 0.3379 dex   (+1.53 sigma from zero)
      unmatched, for comparison: +0.0057 +- 0.1538 -- the matching moves it by +0.512 dex, which is the size of the systematic the control caught.
  [PASS] k-hzs-1d THE DIFFERENTIAL ITSELF, gas-fraction matched: with the estimator's own bias cancelled by construction AND the gas-fraction dependence of that bias removed by reweighting, a_0 at z ~ 1 must equal a_0 at z = 0 if the framework's constant-a_0 law holds.  The check is that the matched difference is within 2 sigma of zero   (matched +0.5178 +- 0.3379 dex = +1.53 sigma; a_0 ~ cH(z) would require +0.241 dex, which is +0.82 sigma away)

    AND A WEIGHT-FREE VERSION OF THE SAME MATCHING, because the reweighting above puts most of its
    weight on SPARC's 25 most gas-rich galaxies.  Here both samples are cut to a COMMON f_gas window:
          f_gas window  N local  N high-z     a0 local       a0 z~1  difference      +-
             0.55-0.85       46        31    3.574e-11    3.528e-11      -0.006   0.202
             0.50-0.90       55        43    3.184e-11    3.848e-11      +0.082   0.163
             0.60-0.80       35        17    3.438e-11    4.598e-11      +0.126   0.282
  [FAIL] k-hzs-1d2 THE WINDOW VERSION must agree with the reweighted version, or the matching itself is driving the answer.  The check is that the two constructions of the SAME quantity agree within the larger of their two error bars   (reweighted +0.518 +- 0.338; windows [0.55,0.85] -0.006 +- 0.202, [0.50,0.90] +0.082 +- 0.163, [0.60,0.80] +0.126 +- 0.282)
  [FAIL] k-hzs-1e AND THE POWER OF THE TEST, stated so it cannot be over-read: the matched differential's error bar must be small enough to see the rival branches.  The check is that it separates the framework's flat law from a_0 ~ cH(z) (+0.241 dex over this baseline) at 2 sigma or better   (band 0.338 dex against a 0.241 dex separation -> cH(z) is +0.82 sigma; the LambdaCDM-native rise over the same baseline (+0.131 dex) is +1.14 sigma and is NOT separated)

