    'implied sig_Mbar' is what the observed scatter would require if ALL of it were baryonic-mass
    error: sd(r)/<1+m>.  If a_0 is one constant and the catalogues' errors are honest, this column
    must be a plausible mass error (0.1-0.3 dex) in EVERY survey, at every redshift.
  [PASS] k-hzs-2 THE CANDIDATE LAW, tested: with a_0 fixed by Lambda and the weight (1+m) fixed by the kernel, each survey's observed residual scatter is compared with what its own quoted errors produce.  The check is that no survey shows more than 2x the scatter its errors allow   (observed/predicted sd(r) = SPARC resolved g_bar 1.13, SPARC degraded to M_bar+R_e 1.55, MUSE-DARK II lensed 1.96, RC100 1.39, MSA-3D golden, stars only 0.83, KROSS stars only 1.97; worst 1.97)
  [FAIL] k-hzs-2b AND THE CROSS-SURVEY VERSION, which is the Kepler-shaped form: the implied baryonic-mass scatter must be ONE plausible number across five samples spanning z = 0 to 2.5.  The check is that it is (a) in the plausible range 0.05-0.40 dex everywhere and (b) consistent to within a factor 2   (implied sigma_Mbar spans 0.178 - 0.550 dex, ratio 3.09)

==========================================================================================================================
PART 3 -- a_0(z) read from the residual: censoring-free, per survey, and jointly
==========================================================================================================================
    The slope is fitted NONLINEARLY on the residual itself: log10 a_0(z) = c + s z, minimising
    sum r_i^2 with r_i = log g_obs - log[nu(g_bar/a_0(z_i)) g_bar].  No per-galaxy division by m,
    which blows up for the high-y galaxies, and no censoring.
    sample                                     d log a0/dz      +-  sigma from flat
    SPARC resolved g_bar (z=0)                          --      --(no redshift lever)
    SPARC degraded to M_bar+R_e (z=0)                   --      --(no redshift lever)
    MUSE-DARK II lensed (z 0.5-1.45)                -1.448   0.363            -3.99
    RC100 (z 0.6-2.5)                               -0.047   0.108            -0.43
    MSA-3D golden, stars only (z 0.6-1.7)           -0.102   0.360            -0.28
    KROSS stars only (z~0.9)                        +0.593   0.994            +0.60

    joint, one free LEVEL per survey and one common slope (the only defensible combination once
    the levels disagree -- k02 Part 5's design, now censoring-free).  MSA-3D and KROSS are
    EXCLUDED from the joint fit: their tables carry no gas, so their g_bar is a lower bound and
    their level is not on the same footing as the others.
      common d log a_0/dz = -0.3069 +- 0.1864   (N = 195, 2 surveys: MUSE-DARK II lensed, RC100)
        level(MUSE-DARK II lensed (z 0.5-1.45)      ) -> a_0(z=0) = 9.790e-11
        level(RC100 (z 0.6-2.5)                     ) -> a_0(z=0) = 5.274e-10
        FRAMEWORK, a_0 constant            predicts +0.000 dex/z  ->   -1.65 sigma
        LambdaCDM-native emergent rise     predicts +0.131 dex/z  ->   -2.35 sigma
        a_0 ~ c H(z) over 0.5<z<2.5        predicts +0.246 dex/z  ->   -2.97 sigma
        MUSE-DARK III (Ciocan+2026)        predicts +0.295 dex/z  ->   -3.23 sigma

    THE CONTROL k03 SHOWED IS DECISIVE, redone here on the uncensored statistic: MUSE-DARK II's own
    gas fraction rises with z BY CONSTRUCTION (Tacconi+20 and NUM scaling relations), so the slope
    must be refitted with the stellar share of g_bar partialled out of z.
      raw                       d log a_0/dz = -1.448 +- 0.353 (-4.10 sigma)
      f_star partialled out of z             = -0.532 +- 0.450 (-1.18 sigma)
      (k03 measured the same collapse on the CENSORED statistic: -0.645 +- 0.273 raw -> +0.016
       +- 0.253 fully controlled.  The uncensored slope is steeper, exactly as k03 predicted it
       would be, because censoring removes the low-a_0 galaxies preferentially at high z.)
  [FAIL] k-hzs-3c THE GAS-FRACTION CONTROL on the censoring-free slope: MUSE-DARK II's apparent decline must survive removing the one variable that rises with z by construction in its own catalogue.  The check is that the controlled slope stays more than 2 sigma from zero   (raw -1.448 +- 0.353 -> controlled -0.532 +- 0.450; the decline is the sample's own gas-scaling relation, not a_0)
  [PASS] k-hzs-3 MUTATION (within-survey shuffle of the per-galaxy a_0): permuting inside each survey must destroy the common slope.  If it does not, the slope is the survey ladder and not redshift   (real -0.3069 +- 0.1864; shuffled +0.0008 +- 0.1118)
  [PASS] k-hzs-3b THE CENSORING-FREE a_0(z), and the check that can fail is that it agrees with the framework's flat law within 2 sigma while separating from at least one rival   (common slope -0.3069 +- 0.1864 = -1.65 sigma from flat; LambdaCDM-native rise at -2.35 sigma, cH(z) at -2.97, MUSE-DARK III at -3.23)

