  [PASS] k-hzs-4 THE UPSILON LEVER, measured by re-running the pipeline at Upsilon x 1.5 (not asserted): the check that can fail is that the residual SCATTER is at least 3x less sensitive to Upsilon than the LEVEL is, in every sample   (|lever| level vs scatter: SPARC resolved g_bar 1.52/0.01, SPARC degraded to M_bar+R_e 2.02/0.06, MUSE-DARK II lensed 1.10/0.12, RC100 5.11/0.05, MSA-3D golden, stars only 1.20/0.04, KROSS stars only 5.84/0.04)

==========================================================================================================================
PART 5 -- the LambdaCDM alternative computed beside the framework
==========================================================================================================================
    RC100: the framework predicts f_DM(<R_e) = 1 - 1/nu(g_bar/a_0) from the BARYONS ALONE, with no
    halo, no concentration and no free parameter.  Measured against the survey's own f_DM:
      mean predicted 0.268 vs mean observed 0.324; rms residual 0.160; corr +0.650
    LambdaCDM makes NO zero-parameter prediction here -- f_DM inside R_e depends on halo mass,
    concentration, and the baryon distribution, all free -- so the comparison is one-sided by
    construction and is reported as such, not as a win.

    The honest LambdaCDM-side statement, and it is against the framework (k02 Part 8): read as f_DM
    the SAME data carry a lever of order 0.3-0.7 to the mass calibration, against LAMBDA = 1.5-3.4
    for a_0.  LambdaCDM's variable is the more stable one; the framework's compensation is that its
    variable is supposed to be a CONSTANT, so its scatter is a test and f_DM's is not.

==========================================================================================================================
PART 6 -- the restatement test, executed, and both footings
==========================================================================================================================
    Attempt: derive sd(r)^2 = sigma_v^2 + (1+m)^2 sigma_M^2 from v^4 = G M_b a_0 plus algebra.
    In the deep limit m -> -1/2, so (1+m) -> 1/2 -- a NUMBER, not a function.  The deep limit
    therefore gives the weight 1/2 for every survey at every acceleration and CANNOT reproduce the
    measured spread of (1+m) across the archive.  Numerically:
      sample                                    <(1+m)> measured  deep-limit value   dex apart
      SPARC resolved g_bar (z=0)                           0.632             0.500      +0.102
      SPARC degraded to M_bar+R_e (z=0)                    0.658             0.500      +0.119
      MUSE-DARK II lensed (z 0.5-1.45)                     0.652             0.500      +0.115
      RC100 (z 0.6-2.5)                                    0.774             0.500      +0.190
      MSA-3D golden, stars only (z 0.6-1.7)                0.637             0.500      +0.105
      KROSS stars only (z~0.9)                             0.744             0.500      +0.173
  [PASS] k-hzs-6 THE RESTATEMENT TEST, executed rather than asserted: the deep-MOND law v^4 = G M_b a_0 fixes (1+m) = 1/2 identically, so it cannot produce the weights this candidate uses.  The check is that the measured weights differ from the deep-limit 1/2 by more than 0.05 dex in at least half the samples -- if they did not, this WOULD be the BTFR in disguise   (|log10(<1+m>/0.5)| = 0.102, 0.119, 0.115, 0.190, 0.105, 0.173)

    HOWEVER -- and this is the honest half -- the relation is derivable from the RAR itself: r, m
    and LAMBDA are all functions of nu, so the candidate is a THEOREM ABOUT MEASUREMENT inside the
    RAR, not an independent regularity.  It is not the BTFR restated (the weights differ), but it
    is the RAR's own error propagation.  Labelled a restatement of the RAR's error budget.

    BOTH FOOTINGS:
    sample                                    d log a0 vs canon  d log a0 vs alt  sd r canon  sd r alt
    SPARC resolved g_bar (z=0)                           +0.021           -0.061       0.139     0.140
    SPARC degraded to M_bar+R_e (z=0)                    -0.406           -0.488       0.189     0.189
    MUSE-DARK II lensed (z 0.5-1.45)                     -0.400           -0.482       0.359     0.357
    RC100 (z 0.6-2.5)                                    +0.218           +0.137       0.213     0.209
    MSA-3D golden, stars only (z 0.6-1.7)                +1.292           +1.211       0.190     0.187
    KROSS stars only (z~0.9)                             -0.006           -0.088       0.406     0.404
  [PASS] k-hzs-6b both footings carried on every number: the SAME measured a_0 is quoted against two different predictions, so the two columns must differ by exactly the footings' own separation.  This check fails if the estimator is not doing that   (footing separation log10(alt/canonical) = 0.0818 dex, recovered exactly in every sample; the measured level is footing-independent and only its INTERPRETATION differs)

